[finding] f-025 The 475 keV and 557 keV neutrino lines differ because of how the captured population in the Sun is treated, not because of the halo. 2609.01590 simulates a non-
**Finding:** The 475 keV and 557 keV neutrino lines differ because of how the captured population in the Sun is treated, not because of the halo. 2609.01590 simulates a non-thermal population on extended solar orbits at m_chi = 1.11 TeV with no elastic cooling (elastic blind spot) and 10-year IceCube data, using <sigma v>_eff of about 1.5e-26 cm^3/s with Sommerfeld; this gives delta_m >~ 475 keV excluded at 90% CL (466 keV with loose solar/nuclear inputs, about 454 keV with the Fe nuclear-response uncertainty). If elastic scattering allows thermalization and capture-annihilation equilibrium, the same paper gets delta_m >~ 553 keV. 2609.07807 assumes thermalization within the Sun after capture, a standard truncated Maxwellian (u0 = 220 km/s, v_esc = 544 km/s, v_sun = 232 km/s), rho = 0.4 GeV/cm^3, sigma_chi_n = 7.4e-39 cm^2, <sigma v> = 1.3e-26 cm^3/s, m_chi = 1.08 TeV and a 95% CL chi^2 on IceCube nu_mu (2016), IceCube nu_e and Super-K data. It excludes delta_m <~ 557 keV and states the interpretation could remain viable with an LMC tail or substructure.
