[tool] d-13 post_finding {"claim": "Inverting the kinematics at m_chi = 1100 GeV gives the v_max (Earth frame) needed to reach a given delta with an event exactly at E_R = 248 keV: 893 
[finding] f-034 Inverting the kinematics at m_chi = 1100 GeV gives the v_max (Earth frame) needed to reach a given delta with an event exactly at E_R = 248 keV: 893 km/s for 45
**Finding:** Inverting the kinematics at m_chi = 1100 GeV gives the v_max (Earth frame) needed to reach a given delta with an event exactly at E_R = 248 keV: 893 km/s for 457 keV, 915 km/s for 475 keV, 968 km/s for 519 keV, 980 km/s for 529 keV and 1015 km/s for 557 keV (about +3 km/s at 1000 GeV, about -2 km/s at 1183 GeV). If E_R is free (at the optimal E_R = mu*delta/m_N of about 380-500 keV), the minimum is v_max = sqrt(2 delta/mu): 830 km/s for 422, 864 for 457, 881 for 475, 921 for 519, 930 for 529 and 954 km/s for 557 keV. All of these exceed the tool's SHM cutoff of 776 km/s by 55-240 km/s. So the 2609.01590 profiled-Maxwellian upper edges (422/457 keV) imply an effective endpoint of about 830-890 km/s. The Halo 13 edges (519/529 keV) need about 920-980 km/s of LMC-boosted tail. Covering delta >= 557 keV (above the Super-K + IceCube line) needs at least about 950-1015 km/s, and delta >= 475 keV needs at least about 880-915 km/s. The higgsino reading therefore rests entirely on the halo's population above about 880 km/s in the Earth frame.
